WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.282
Picture a busy Friday night in a restaurant.

2
00:00:05.282 --> 00:00:08.699
The manager's clipboard says four waiters are on the floor.

3
00:00:08.867 --> 00:00:09.897
And that's true.

4
00:00:09.897 --> 00:00:10.928
But look closer.

5
00:00:10.928 --> 00:00:15.244
One of them has stood frozen at the coffee machine for ten minutes.

6
00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:19.204
Another can't take orders, because their card reader is broken.

7
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:22.301
The count is right, and guests are still waiting.

8
00:00:22.301 --> 00:00:25.024
Being on the floor is not the same as working.

9
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.596
Now the same scene in Kubernetes.

10
00:00:27.596 --> 00:00:29.991
It tells you your Pod is Running.

11
00:00:29.991 --> 00:00:31.298
So your app works.

12
00:00:31.298 --> 00:00:31.733
Right?

13
00:00:31.933 --> 00:00:34.764
That's the question that feels too silly to ask.

14
00:00:34.764 --> 00:00:36.533
Let's see if it's really true.

15
00:00:36.733 --> 00:00:40.036
Kubernetes already handles one kind of trouble.

16
00:00:40.036 --> 00:00:46.853
Since Day 39, when a container's process, its running program, exits, Kubernetes starts it again.

17
00:00:46.853 --> 00:00:48.188
A crash is handled.

18
00:00:48.333 --> 00:00:50.392
But our two waiters did not crash.

19
00:00:50.392 --> 00:00:52.209
They are still standing there.

20
00:00:52.209 --> 00:00:55.479
From the outside, they look exactly like healthy ones.

21
00:00:55.667 --> 00:01:02.639
Our lab app, hello-devops, is a tiny web app that answers every visit with a greeting and a count of visits.

22
00:01:02.639 --> 00:01:07.029
Last time, it started keeping that count in Redis, a small database.

23
00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.407
So the frozen waiter is a stuck app.

24
00:01:09.407 --> 00:01:15.171
And the broken card reader is an app that's up, but can't reach Redis, so it can't do its job.

25
00:01:15.333 --> 00:01:18.348
Honestly, Running should have been called: has a pulse.

26
00:01:18.533 --> 00:01:20.666
Because that's all it tells you.

27
00:01:20.666 --> 00:01:21.932
The process exists.

28
00:01:21.932 --> 00:01:22.798
Nothing more.

29
00:01:22.798 --> 00:01:25.664
And that's exactly the clipboard's mistake.

30
00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:32.484
Wouldn't it be nice if the manager walked the floor, and actually asked each waiter a question, every so often?

31
00:01:34.267 --> 00:01:36.622
So let's give the manager that habit.

32
00:01:36.622 --> 00:01:39.677
Walk over, ask a question, listen to the answer.

33
00:01:39.867 --> 00:01:40.897
Let's map that.

34
00:01:40.897 --> 00:01:45.020
The manager is the kubelet, the helper program on each node.

35
00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:47.012
The waiter is your container.

36
00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:50.380
The question is a probe: a small check on a timer.

37
00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:55.213
In the lab, the kubelet asks the container for a web address, like /healthz.

38
00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:59.545
And the answer is a status code, the number a web server sends back.

39
00:01:59.733 --> 00:02:04.466
Any code from 200 to 399 counts as a pass.

40
00:02:04.466 --> 00:02:07.171
503 means not available.

41
00:02:07.933 --> 00:02:09.763
But one question isn't enough.

42
00:02:09.763 --> 00:02:15.008
A good manager asks three different ones, and each answer leads to a different action.

43
00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:19.559
First, to a new waiter still tying an apron: have you finished starting?

44
00:02:19.733 --> 00:02:22.926
Until the answer is yes, the other questions wait.

45
00:02:22.926 --> 00:02:26.311
So a slow start is never mistaken for a stuck waiter.

46
00:02:26.311 --> 00:02:30.015
And if the answer never comes, the container is restarted.

47
00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:33.599
Second, to everyone, every so often: are you stuck?

48
00:02:33.733 --> 00:02:39.265
After enough no answers in a row, the frozen waiter goes home, and a fresh one comes in.

49
00:02:39.265 --> 00:02:43.539
In Kubernetes, the kubelet kills the container and starts a new one.

50
00:02:43.733 --> 00:02:46.215
Third: can you take tables right now?

51
00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:49.983
While the answer is no, no new tables go to that waiter.

52
00:02:49.983 --> 00:02:51.327
But nobody goes home.

53
00:02:51.327 --> 00:02:55.295
The waiter with the broken reader stays, and nothing restarts.

54
00:02:55.467 --> 00:02:58.412
Now the names, because now they mean something.

55
00:02:58.412 --> 00:03:00.355
The first is the startup probe.

56
00:03:00.355 --> 00:03:02.297
The second, the liveness probe.

57
00:03:02.297 --> 00:03:04.240
The third, the readiness probe.

58
00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:09.569
Three questions, three actions: wait, restart, or stop sending visitors.

59
00:03:09.733 --> 00:03:12.482
Our app already has two addresses for this.

60
00:03:12.482 --> 00:03:16.765
/healthz answers as long as the Python process can serve a request.

61
00:03:16.933 --> 00:03:21.647
/readyz also checks Redis, and returns 503 when Redis is down.

62
00:03:21.647 --> 00:03:26.589
So startup and liveness ask /healthz, and readiness asks /readyz.

63
00:03:27.333 --> 00:03:29.081
How fast does each one act?

64
00:03:29.081 --> 00:03:32.189
Every probe takes the same four timing settings.

65
00:03:32.333 --> 00:03:33.591
How often to check.

66
00:03:33.591 --> 00:03:35.444
How long one check may take.

67
00:03:35.444 --> 00:03:38.025
How many misses in a row before acting.

68
00:03:38.025 --> 00:03:40.275
And how long to wait at the start.

69
00:03:40.467 --> 00:03:45.273
By default: every ten seconds, one second each, three misses, no wait.

70
00:03:46.067 --> 00:03:48.375
Multiply them, and you get the time.

71
00:03:48.375 --> 00:03:53.312
The lab's startup probe checks every two seconds, and allows thirty failures.

72
00:03:53.312 --> 00:03:57.159
Thirty times two: the app gets up to sixty seconds to start.

73
00:03:57.333 --> 00:03:58.002
Your turn.

74
00:03:58.002 --> 00:04:02.817
Liveness checks every ten seconds, and acts after three misses in a row.

75
00:04:02.817 --> 00:04:05.826
How long does a stuck container keep its job?

76
00:04:05.826 --> 00:04:06.896
Pause and guess.

77
00:04:09.067 --> 00:04:10.518
About thirty seconds.

78
00:04:10.518 --> 00:04:13.005
Miss, miss, miss, and it's replaced.

79
00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:17.116
Readiness checks every five seconds, and acts after two misses.

80
00:04:17.116 --> 00:04:20.659
So a Pod stops getting visitors within about ten seconds.

81
00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:24.304
You might be thinking: why bother with a startup probe?

82
00:04:24.304 --> 00:04:27.235
Just give liveness a long initialDelaySeconds.

83
00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:28.736
That's a fixed guess.

84
00:04:28.736 --> 00:04:35.669
Set it for the slowest start, and liveness is blind that long, after every single start, even the quick ones.

85
00:04:35.867 --> 00:04:39.948
A startup probe hands over to liveness the moment the app answers.

86
00:04:40.733 --> 00:04:43.263
Now let's be generous to a tempting idea.

87
00:04:43.263 --> 00:04:46.347
If checking is good, more checking must be better.

88
00:04:46.347 --> 00:04:48.753
So let's make /healthz check Redis too.

89
00:04:48.933 --> 00:04:51.500
Liveness now asks about Redis as well.

90
00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:53.256
Sounds responsible, right?

91
00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:56.308
Picture Redis going down, for just one minute.

92
00:04:56.467 --> 00:05:00.432
Every hello-devops Pod fails liveness at about the same time.

93
00:05:00.432 --> 00:05:02.837
And the kubelet restarts all of them.

94
00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:04.826
But restarts can't fix Redis.

95
00:05:04.826 --> 00:05:08.351
And while they happen, no Pod can serve anything at all.

96
00:05:08.351 --> 00:05:10.743
A small outage just became a full one.

97
00:05:10.933 --> 00:05:17.579
It's the manager who hears the card network is down for the whole street, and sends every waiter home.

98
00:05:17.579 --> 00:05:18.035
Well...

99
00:05:18.035 --> 00:05:20.511
nobody's card reader is a problem now.

100
00:05:20.667 --> 00:05:22.529
So the point really is this.

101
00:05:22.529 --> 00:05:25.853
A restart only fixes trouble inside the container.

102
00:05:25.853 --> 00:05:33.700
The Kubernetes docs warn that a wrong liveness probe can lead to cascading failures, one failure setting off the next.

103
00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:37.054
Liveness asks about the container alone.

104
00:05:37.054 --> 00:05:38.979
Leave the database out of it.

105
00:05:39.133 --> 00:05:44.601
Checks on other services belong in readiness, because failing it only pauses visitors.

106
00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:47.023
Let's try it the way the lab does.

107
00:05:47.023 --> 00:05:48.330
Scale Redis to zero.

108
00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:52.841
Pause and guess: what happens to READY, and what happens to RESTARTS?

109
00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:56.864
READY drops to zero of one.

110
00:05:56.864 --> 00:05:58.660
STATUS still says Running.

111
00:05:58.660 --> 00:06:00.524
And RESTARTS stays at zero.

112
00:06:00.667 --> 00:06:02.758
That quiet gap is the whole point.

113
00:06:02.758 --> 00:06:04.604
The waiters stay on the floor.

114
00:06:04.604 --> 00:06:06.511
They just don't get new tables.

115
00:06:06.667 --> 00:06:09.015
How does a failed check stop visitors?

116
00:06:09.015 --> 00:06:13.713
The kubelet writes the result into the Pod's status, as its Ready condition.

117
00:06:13.713 --> 00:06:19.956
Kubernetes copies that onto the Service's list of Pods, and traffic skips the Pods that aren't ready.

118
00:06:20.133 --> 00:06:24.771
Scale Redis back to one, and the Pods become ready again, on their own.

119
00:06:24.771 --> 00:06:26.077
Nobody touched them.

120
00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:27.959
One honest limit.

121
00:06:27.959 --> 00:06:29.867
Readiness isn't free either.

122
00:06:29.867 --> 00:06:35.865
If every Pod fails it at once, the Service has no ready Pods left, and users get errors.

123
00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:39.333
That's exactly what you'd see while Redis was at zero.

124
00:06:39.333 --> 00:06:41.987
Every Pod stepped aside at the same moment.

125
00:06:42.133 --> 00:06:44.842
So the lab doesn't pick the tightest values.

126
00:06:44.842 --> 00:06:49.891
Its readiness probe allows a two second timeout, and two failures, before it acts.

127
00:06:50.667 --> 00:06:52.500
So, the moral of the story.

128
00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:55.283
Being present is not the same as working.

129
00:06:55.283 --> 00:06:57.659
Startup says: wait until I'm ready.

130
00:06:57.659 --> 00:07:00.646
Liveness says: go home, and come back fresh.

131
00:07:00.646 --> 00:07:03.361
Readiness says: step aside for a moment.

132
00:07:03.361 --> 00:07:06.280
And liveness never asks about the database.

133
00:07:07.067 --> 00:07:09.071
One quick check before you go.

134
00:07:09.071 --> 00:07:14.883
An app needs ninety seconds to start, and liveness kills it at thirty, again and again.

135
00:07:14.883 --> 00:07:16.286
Which probe fixes it?

136
00:07:16.286 --> 00:07:17.355
Pause and think.

137
00:07:19.533 --> 00:07:22.878
A startup probe that allows more than ninety seconds.

138
00:07:22.878 --> 00:07:25.971
It holds liveness back until the app has started.

139
00:07:25.971 --> 00:07:27.233
Thanks for watching.
